1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:03,450
Hello, and welcome back
to the Articulate Journal.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,760
Articulate is a public engineering
journal documenting the design and

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,780
implementation of an AI-native
architecture platform.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:18,060
Every episode explores another
architectural question, and together they

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:19,520
explain how the system evolves.

6
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:23,910
In the previous video episode, I
talked about the characteristics

7
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:27,710
that every production system needs,
whether it's AI-native or not.

8
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:32,860
Qualities like reliability, scalability,
security and observability don't

9
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:35,110
disappear just because we introduce AI.

10
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:36,970
They become even more important.

11
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:40,190
In this episode, I'd like
to turn our attention to

12
00:00:40,230 --> 00:00:43,840
the conceptual data model that
will hold architectural knowledge.

13
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:47,920
Before I can build AI that helps
architects understand architecture,

14
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,940
I first need to answer this question.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,990
What is architectural knowledge?
At first, I thought the answer

16
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:58,730
would be obvious. Architecture is
made up of diagrams, documents,

17
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:02,980
Architecture Decision Records, source
code, infrastructure definitions…

18
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,520
all the things we create
throughout the lifetime of a

19
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,850
system. But the more I
thought about it, the more

20
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:12,280
I realised those things
aren't actually the knowledge.

21
00:01:12,537 --> 00:01:19,210
That is just information. Useful,
certainly, but information nonetheless.

22
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,900
Imagine joining a new
organisation as an architect.

23
00:01:21,995 --> 00:01:24,270
On your first day you're given
access to the architecture

24
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:27,440
repository. There are dozens of diagrams.

25
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:29,160
Hundreds of pages of documentation.

26
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:31,910
A collection of ADRs. Wikis.

27
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:35,730
Slide decks. Maybe even recordings
from previous design reviews.

28
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:38,810
You now have a huge amount of information.

29
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:40,880
But do you understand the architecture?

30
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:42,630
Probably not.

31
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,850
Understanding doesn't come
from reading one document.

32
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:49,810
It comes from gradually connecting
all of those pieces together.

33
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:52,650
You begin to see how the
systems relate to one

34
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:55,780
another. Why particular
decisions were made.

35
00:01:56,210 --> 00:01:58,810
Which principles shaped the architecture.

36
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:02,770
Where the risks are. Which
assumptions are still valid.

37
00:02:03,190 --> 00:02:06,800
And which parts of the architecture
have evolved over time.

38
00:02:07,210 --> 00:02:10,440
That's when information
starts to become knowledge.

39
00:02:11,210 --> 00:02:18,370
That distinction became one
of the most important ideas in

40
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:22,270
the design of Articulate.
Because if I simply build a

41
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:23,450
platform that stores documents, then
I've really just created another

42
00:02:23,490 --> 00:02:26,960
document repository. What I want
to model is the understanding

43
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,760
that emerges from those documents.

44
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,840
Not just what the architecture says.

45
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:35,770
But what the architecture means.

46
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:40,100
That became the foundation for
everything that follows in this

47
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:40,770
episode.

48
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:45,750
Once I'd started thinking about
architectural knowledge, another question

49
00:02:45,790 --> 00:02:49,710
naturally followed. What information
actually exists within an architecture?

50
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:50,910
At first glance, it seems obvious.

51
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:53,580
We have systems. Applications. Services.

52
00:02:53,790 --> 00:02:59,830
Databases. APIs. Infrastructure.
Those are certainly part of the

53
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:06,580
architecture. But they're
only part of the picture.

54
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,100
Architecture also includes the
principles that guide decisions.

55
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:09,850
The characteristics the
system needs to exhibit.

56
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:12,790
The decisions that have
been made over time.

57
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:16,800
The relationships between
different parts of the estate.

58
00:03:16,990 --> 00:03:19,080
The assumptions people are working with.

59
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:22,730
The constraints they
have to operate within.

60
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:25,810
And the evidence that
supports those decisions.

61
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:30,710
Taken together, that's a
huge amount of information.

62
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:33,100
Some of it is highly structured.

63
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,740
Some of it is buried inside documents.

64
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:39,610
Some of it only exists because
somebody remembers why a

65
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,770
decision was made three years ago.

66
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:45,170
The challenge isn't finding
places to store that information.

67
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:48,980
The challenge is bringing
it together in a way that

68
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:50,780
allows us to reason about it.

69
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:54,160
That brought me to another
important distinction.

70
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:59,080
The difference between
information and knowledge.

71
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:05,320
Suppose Articulate knows that a
payment service uses PostgreSQL.

72
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,160
On its own, that's simply
a piece of information.

73
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:13,950
Now suppose it also knows that
the service processes financial

74
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:18,120
transactions. That high
availability is a characteristic.

75
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:24,720
That an Architecture Decision Record
explains why PostgreSQL was selected.

76
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:28,000
That another proposal to move
to a different database was

77
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:33,580
rejected. And that recent operational
issues suggest the original

78
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:35,750
assumptions may no longer hold.

79
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:39,440
Individually, each of those is
just another piece of information.

80
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:44,580
But once they're connected together,
something interesting happens.

81
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:48,080
The platform doesn't
just know isolated facts.

82
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,770
It begins to understand the
context surrounding them.

83
00:04:52,210 --> 00:04:55,160
That's much closer to what
I think of as knowledge.

84
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:58,960
After thinking about this for
quite a while, I eventually

85
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:01,720
arrived at a very simple definition.

86
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:05,440
Knowledge is information
combined with thinking.

87
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:07,830
It sounds almost too simple.

88
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,410
But that single idea changed
how I thought about the

89
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:15,100
entire platform. The goal isn't
to collect as much information

90
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,130
as possible. The goal
is to build a model that

91
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:23,060
captures understanding. Because that's
what architects actually work with

92
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:23,810
every day.

93
00:05:32,190 --> 00:05:32,690
Once I'd reached the conclusion that
knowledge is information combined

94
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:33,230
with thinking, another question emerged.

95
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:34,860
How should that knowledge
actually be represented?

96
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:38,960
The more I explored the
problem, the more I realised

97
00:05:39,190 --> 00:05:41,740
that almost everything we know
about an architecture can be

98
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:45,670
expressed as a claim. A
claim is simply an assertion

99
00:05:45,710 --> 00:05:49,470
about the architecture. For example…

100
00:05:49,570 --> 00:05:54,890
Order service uses PostgreSQL.
Checkout capability requires high

101
00:05:54,930 --> 00:06:00,760
availability. Product API is
consumed by three downstream systems.

102
00:06:01,210 --> 00:06:13,800
architectural decision 16 was
made to improve resilience.

103
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:15,460
Each of those statements tells us
something about the architecture.

104
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:16,000
But on their own, they're
just isolated assertions.

105
00:06:16,040 --> 00:06:19,750
What makes them useful is the
context that surrounds them.

106
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:21,710
Where did this claim come from?

107
00:06:21,850 --> 00:06:23,790
Was it discovered from source code?

108
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:26,840
Taken from an Architecture
Decision Record?

109
00:06:27,410 --> 00:06:29,730
Suggested during a
conversation with an architect?

110
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:33,140
Or generated by an AI during discovery?

111
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:36,790
How confident are we that it's correct?

112
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:41,170
What evidence supports it?
Has it always been true, or

113
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:43,800
has the architecture evolved over time?

114
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:47,800
Those questions become just as
important as the claim itself.

115
00:06:48,210 --> 00:06:53,110
Rather than storing disconnected facts,
the Knowledge Model stores claims

116
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:56,860
together with the information needed
to understand and evaluate them.

117
00:06:57,250 --> 00:06:59,980
That allows the model to grow over time.

118
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:03,720
New evidence can strengthen
an existing claim.

119
00:07:04,210 --> 00:07:06,780
Conflicting evidence can challenge it.

120
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:10,570
And as the architecture evolves,
the understanding can evolve with

121
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:13,060
it. I think that's a much
better reflection of how

122
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:14,810
architects work in the real world.

123
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,800
We rarely have perfect knowledge.

124
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:20,110
Instead, we gradually build
confidence as more information becomes

125
00:07:20,290 --> 00:07:24,450
available. The Knowledge Model isn't
trying to eliminate uncertainty.

126
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:27,960
It's trying to make uncertainty explicit.

127
00:07:28,050 --> 00:07:30,140
And I think that's a much
more honest representation of

128
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:30,730
architecture.

129
00:07:31,190 --> 00:07:34,730
By this point, I had the
beginnings of a model.

130
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:38,370
I had architectural information.
I had relationships between that

131
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:41,750
information. And I had claims
that represented what the system

132
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:44,790
currently understood
about the architecture.

133
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:47,430
But something still
didn't feel quite right.

134
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:51,220
A collection of connected claims
is certainly more useful than

135
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:53,580
a collection of disconnected documents.

136
00:07:54,210 --> 00:07:56,080
But it still isn't knowledge.

137
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:59,770
Knowledge comes from
interpreting those claims.

138
00:07:59,970 --> 00:08:03,860
It comes from understanding
how they relate to one another.

139
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:06,890
How they support or contradict each other.

140
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:08,860
How confident we are in them.

141
00:08:09,210 --> 00:08:12,010
And what they actually
mean in the context of the

142
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:13,780
wider architecture.

143
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,960
For example, imagine Articulate
discovers that a service has suffered

144
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:26,760
several operational incidents. On
its own, that's just another claim.

145
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:29,990
But if it also understands
that the service supports a

146
00:08:30,030 --> 00:08:35,140
business-critical capability… That
reliability is one of the architectural

147
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:39,710
principles… That an earlier ADR
accepted a trade-off in favour

148
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:43,530
of performance… And that the
current maturity of that area

149
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:47,010
is relatively low… Then the
platform can begin to reason

150
00:08:47,050 --> 00:08:49,820
about the significance
of those observations.

151
00:08:49,970 --> 00:08:52,690
It isn't simply retrieving information.

152
00:08:52,730 --> 00:08:57,490
It's building understanding. That,
to me, is the difference between

153
00:08:57,530 --> 00:09:00,750
an information model
and a knowledge model.

154
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:05,170
The purpose of the Knowledge Model
isn't to replace architectural

155
00:09:05,210 --> 00:09:10,480
judgement. It's to provide enough
connected understanding that both humans

156
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:14,580
and AI can reason about the
architecture more effectively.

157
00:09:15,210 --> 00:09:18,880
That's the point where architecture
starts to become more than

158
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:22,780
a collection of documents. It
becomes a body of knowledge

159
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:27,320
that can grow, evolve and support
better architectural decisions over

160
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:27,860
time.

161
00:09:28,010 --> 00:09:31,550
By this point, I felt I
had enough understanding to

162
00:09:31,590 --> 00:09:34,730
describe what the Knowledge
Model actually is.

163
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:37,970
It's important to remember that
I'm still not thinking about

164
00:09:38,010 --> 00:09:42,160
databases or implementation. There's
one piece of terminology I'd like

165
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:46,660
to explain. Throughout this episode
I've deliberately referred to it

166
00:09:46,700 --> 00:09:49,740
as a Knowledge Model,
not a Knowledge Graph.

167
00:09:50,210 --> 00:09:53,780
That choice is intentional.
It may well turn out that

168
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:56,800
a graph is the best way to implement it.

169
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:59,710
But at this stage, I don't want
to introduce implementation

170
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:01,730
bias into the architectural thinking.

171
00:10:02,210 --> 00:10:06,020
Right now, I'm trying to
understand the concepts that need

172
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:09,080
to be represented. The
structure of the knowledge.

173
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:14,240
The relationships. The behaviour.
Only once I understand the conceptual

174
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:17,560
model do I want to start
asking how it should

175
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:22,450
be implemented. That implementation
might involve a graph database.

176
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:26,820
It might involve several different
technologies working together.

177
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:28,800
I simply don't know yet.

178
00:10:28,930 --> 00:10:33,810
And that's perfectly fine. I'm
thinking about the conceptual model.

179
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:38,580
The Knowledge Model is the way
Articulate represents architectural

180
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:43,440
understanding. It brings together the
architectural concepts we've explored

181
00:10:43,510 --> 00:10:46,940
throughout this episode.
Structure. Characteristics.

182
00:10:46,980 --> 00:10:50,800
Principles. Decisions. And
the claims that connect them.

183
00:10:51,210 --> 00:10:55,740
Each claim captures something the
platform currently understands about the

184
00:10:55,780 --> 00:11:00,950
architecture. Together, those claims
form a connected body of knowledge

185
00:11:00,990 --> 00:11:02,900
rather than a collection
of disconnected facts.

186
00:11:03,250 --> 00:11:05,960
What I particularly like about
this approach is that the

187
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,010
model can evolve. As new
information is discovered…

188
00:11:09,050 --> 00:11:11,660
As decisions are made… As systems change…

189
00:11:11,820 --> 00:11:14,740
And as more evidence becomes available…

190
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:17,850
The model evolves alongside
the architecture itself.

191
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:21,770
Nothing needs to be treated
as permanently fixed.

192
00:11:22,210 --> 00:11:25,780
The understanding simply
becomes richer over time.

193
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:30,380
That also means the Knowledge
Model becomes much more than

194
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:32,890
somewhere to store
architectural information.

195
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:37,040
It becomes the foundation that
every other capability in Articulate

196
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:40,620
builds upon. Discovery adds to it.

197
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:42,820
Conversations retrieve knowledge from it.

198
00:11:43,210 --> 00:11:45,740
Impact analysis reasons over it.

199
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:48,810
Architecture decisions become part of it.

200
00:11:49,210 --> 00:11:51,930
Future AI agents use it
as context for their own

201
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:55,940
reasoning. Instead of every capability
maintaining its own understanding of

202
00:11:55,980 --> 00:11:59,970
the architecture, they all work
from the same shared model.

203
00:12:00,210 --> 00:12:03,010
For me, that's one of the most
important architectural ideas

204
00:12:03,050 --> 00:12:07,000
in Articulate. The Knowledge Model
becomes the system's shared understanding

205
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:10,890
of the architecture. Everything
else is simply another way of

206
00:12:10,930 --> 00:12:15,640
contributing to that understanding,
reasoning over it, or presenting it

207
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:16,740
to the user.

208
00:12:21,250 --> 00:12:21,750
Once I had the Knowledge Model,
another question naturally followed.

209
00:12:21,790 --> 00:12:25,760
How does the rest of the
platform interact with it?

210
00:12:25,970 --> 00:12:28,740
My answer was to
introduce a Knowledge API.

211
00:12:29,200 --> 00:12:31,930
The purpose of the API
isn't simply to read and

212
00:12:31,970 --> 00:12:35,220
write data. Its purpose is
to provide a consistent way

213
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:38,110
for every part of the platform
to work with architectural

214
00:12:38,290 --> 00:12:40,800
knowledge. Discovery can add new claims.

215
00:12:41,210 --> 00:12:44,500
Conversations can retrieve
architectural context.

216
00:12:45,210 --> 00:12:48,140
Reasoning services can
explore relationships.

217
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:51,750
Impact analysis can
understand dependencies.

218
00:12:52,210 --> 00:12:55,950
Future agents can query the
model without needing to know

219
00:12:55,990 --> 00:12:59,800
how it's stored internally.
That separation is important.

220
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:02,230
The rest of the platform shouldn't
care whether the Knowledge

221
00:13:02,270 --> 00:13:05,855
Model is backed by a graph
database, a document database,

222
00:13:05,895 --> 00:13:07,950
a relational database, or
something else entirely.

223
00:13:08,210 --> 00:13:13,960
Those are implementation details. The
capabilities of the platform interact

224
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,550
with the Knowledge API. The
Knowledge API interacts with the

225
00:13:18,590 --> 00:13:22,060
Knowledge Model. That gives
me the freedom to evolve the

226
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:25,780
implementation without changing the
architecture built on top of it.

227
00:13:26,210 --> 00:13:30,200
More importantly, it establishes
the Knowledge Model as a first-class

228
00:13:30,370 --> 00:13:35,050
architectural capability. It isn't
just another database hidden behind a

229
00:13:35,090 --> 00:13:39,410
service. It's the shared understanding
of the architecture, exposed through

230
00:13:39,450 --> 00:13:43,800
a well-defined interface that every
other capability can build upon.

231
00:13:44,190 --> 00:13:47,990
As the platform evolves,
I expect the API to evolve

232
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:51,810
as well. New ways of querying
knowledge will emerge.

233
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:54,830
New reasoning capabilities
will be introduced.

234
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:56,800
The model itself will become richer.

235
00:13:57,180 --> 00:13:59,830
But the architectural
idea remains the same.

236
00:14:00,210 --> 00:14:02,720
There is one Knowledge Model.

237
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:05,210
And there is one consistent
way for the rest of

238
00:14:05,250 --> 00:14:07,800
the platform to understand
and contribute to it.

239
00:14:08,190 --> 00:14:10,930
Stepping back from the details
of the Knowledge Model, I

240
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:15,730
realised something interesting. What
I'm really trying to build isn't

241
00:14:15,770 --> 00:14:19,860
another architecture repository.
It isn't another modelling tool.

242
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:23,090
And it certainly isn't another
AI chatbot that happens to

243
00:14:23,130 --> 00:14:25,780
know something about
software architecture.

244
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:29,050
What I'm exploring is something
I think of as Architectural

245
00:14:29,090 --> 00:14:34,040
Intelligence. The ability for a
system to understand architecture in

246
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:38,580
much the same way an architect
gradually builds understanding over

247
00:14:38,650 --> 00:14:40,650
time. Not by memorising documents.

248
00:14:40,690 --> 00:14:44,920
Not by indexing diagrams. But by
connecting information, evaluating

249
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:48,730
evidence, understanding relationships
and reasoning about change.

250
00:14:49,210 --> 00:14:52,160
That's why the Knowledge
Model is so important.

251
00:14:53,210 --> 00:14:57,170
Without it, AI is simply generating
responses from whatever information

252
00:14:57,510 --> 00:15:00,780
happens to be available.
With it, the platform has a

253
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:03,770
shared understanding
that it can reason over.

254
00:15:04,210 --> 00:15:06,970
That doesn't mean the AI
replaces the architect.

255
00:15:07,010 --> 00:15:10,800
Far from it. The architect
still makes the decisions.

256
00:15:11,210 --> 00:15:13,800
The architect still applies judgement.

257
00:15:14,150 --> 00:15:16,840
The architect is still
accountable for the outcome.

258
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:19,840
The role of the AI is to
help build understanding.

259
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:23,160
To connect ideas that might
otherwise remain disconnected.

260
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:27,140
To surface relationships.
To identify contradictions.

261
00:15:27,700 --> 00:15:30,080
To explain the consequences of change.

262
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:36,660
And to help architects navigate
increasingly complex systems.

263
00:15:37,210 --> 00:15:40,520
Whether that vision is achievable
is something this journal will

264
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:43,850
continue to explore. But I
do believe it starts with

265
00:15:43,890 --> 00:15:47,330
the Knowledge Model. Because
before an AI can help us

266
00:15:47,370 --> 00:15:52,710
reason about architecture, it first
needs a meaningful representation of

267
00:15:52,750 --> 00:15:53,860
what architecture actually is.

268
00:15:54,250 --> 00:15:56,990
This episode has been less
about technology and more about

269
00:15:57,030 --> 00:15:59,790
understanding the problem
I'm trying to solve.

270
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:03,280
Before I can build an AI-native
architecture platform, I first

271
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:06,810
need a way of representing
architectural knowledge.

272
00:16:07,210 --> 00:16:10,160
That journey led from
architectural information…

273
00:16:10,820 --> 00:16:13,970
To connected claims… To a Knowledge Model…

274
00:16:14,130 --> 00:16:16,730
And ultimately to the idea of
Architectural Intelligence.

275
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:20,850
Now that I have a conceptual
model for architectural knowledge,

276
00:16:21,130 --> 00:16:26,420
another question naturally follows.
How does AI actually reason over

277
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:30,830
it? Some questions might be
answered through semantic search.

278
00:16:31,210 --> 00:16:34,120
Others might require
traversing relationships.

279
00:16:34,490 --> 00:16:37,080
Some might need structured queries.

280
00:16:37,150 --> 00:16:40,820
And some may require several
different approaches working together.

281
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:45,370
In the next episode, we'll begin
exploring how AI retrieves,

282
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:49,080
reasons over and makes use
of the Knowledge Model.

283
00:16:49,210 --> 00:16:51,850
Because building the model is
only half of the challenge.

284
00:16:52,170 --> 00:16:55,000
The real value comes from
helping both humans and AI

285
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:58,770
use that knowledge to make
better architectural decisions.

286
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:01,440
I'll see you in the next episode.
